KZ3 patch 1074 (SPU pass 58; DEFAULT ON since SPU pass 60 with 1062 + 1077, KZ3_SPURS_KERNEL=0 = the old poll
kernel): the SPURS kernel
as one host thread per SPU of the instance, running a workload only while it is wanted, from ps3recomp PR #213
(libs/spurs/spurs_kernel.c, kselect) by NoahBPeterson (MIT). The selection rule and weight are his; the port keeps
this runtime's policy-module runner (spu_run_policy_module, persistent PM contexts per (wid, spuNum)) and its
lock-line stripes, and leaves out the system service, pending contention and the PPU helper threads.
- Thread kt of an instance is SPU kt (spuNum = kt, nSpus threads). Between modules it selects under the instance
  line's stripe lock (runtime/spu/spurs_kern_sched.h kz3_ksel): runnable (enabled, state 2, a module of this
  instance), priority byte for this SPU != 0, contention < maxContention, and wanted (signal, flag, or
  readyCount != 0 and readyCount + idleSpuCount > contention); weight = wanted, priority, last workload, below
  minContention, fewer SPUs, same uniqueId, lowest wid. The pick's contention is raised and its signal taken; the
  module runs on this thread as SPU kt until it returns to the kernel; contention is lowered.
- Nothing wanted: the SPU sleeps on the instance's condition variable, woken by the ReadyCount imports, a PUTLLC
  commit on the instance's lines 0-1, another SPU leaving a workload, RequestIdleSpu and cellSpursWakeUp, and
  re-reads the instance at most 1 ms later for the guest's own inlined stores.
- selectWorkload from a running module (LS 0x9D0) is answered by the same rule with the SPU's own unit left out
  ({wid, status} in r3 words 0/1).
- The ReadyCount imports update the instance byte under the stripe lock and return the instance's old value (the
  WWS PM swaps its readyCount to 0 when its list runs dry and CASes it back, so the host copy was stale), and
  cellSpursRequestIdleSpu(spurs, wid, count) sets idleSpuCount (it was a stub that dropped both arguments). Both
  only with the per-SPU kernel (default; not with KZ3_SPURS_KERNEL=0).
- KZ3_SPURS_IDLE_ADMIT=1 (experiment): idleSpuCount alone admits a workload on an idle SPU. KZ3_KERN_TRACE=n:
  [spurs-ktrace] lines for the first n runs/polls per wid. [spurs-inst] every 10 s: the instance's ready and
  contention bytes.
Why it was opt-in until pass 60 (SPU pass 58, K4 recipe, the RE notes Pass 58; the cause, found in pass 60, was
the PM argument layout fixed by patch 1077: every PM entry PUT FFFF over the list, the RE notes Pass 60): with 5/1 (KZ3_SPURS_ATTR_SDK=1) the boot stalls at
~9-12 s (runs spu-58-gate/-gate2/-gate3/-diag/-diag2): the PPU kicks jm_generic (wid 8, readyCount 8), five SPUs
take it, one runs the only claimable job while the other four find the next job blocked and drain the ready count
to 0 (PM 0x3040), the running PM's next poll is answered "switch" (ready 0), and no job completion re-kicks wid 8:
the list strands and the main thread waits at 0x307148 forever. The old kernel's cadence runs hide this. With
idle admission the SPUs churn on wid 7 instead (14 flips). Host test tools/spu/test_spurs_kern.sh.

Rebased on ps3recomp v0.18.0 (SPU pass 108): context only (upstream v0.18.0 changed lines around the hunks; same change).

